================================================================================
 WHAT THE RUNS ABOVE ESTABLISH
 Two suite invocations, six timed runs, every ratio formed inside one process.
 (The three runs appended last are the FIRST invocation's, kept so the spread is
 across two builds' worth of runs and not one.)
================================================================================

1. CORRECTNESS, AND IT IS THE GATE FOR EVERYTHING ELSE

   16 payloads over 7 roots, four decoders each: the independent `armonik` arm
   (prost's own codec over the facade's prost::Message impls), the push arm, and
   the two pull arms.  All agree on the facade value, everywhere.  M7 is in the
   set, which matters here: its bytes interleave two repeated fields of one type,
   so it is the payload that exercises section 7.3's flush on a foreign tag --
   emitted by the traversal the two families SHARE, and therefore inherited by
   pull rather than reimplemented in it.

   The push family's own gate (byte identity across all four arms against the
   validated manifest, every payload) is re-run in section 2 of the transcript
   and still passes.

2. THE STRUCTURAL CONTROL, AND IT IS THE ANSWER TO DECISION 2's SECOND HALF

   A record is written exactly where the push family makes a reverse call, by
   construction, so the two counts must be equal.  They are, TO THE DIGIT, on all
   thirteen counted payloads:

     P1.1 2/2   P1.2 5/5   P1.3 3/3   P2.1 8/8   P2.2 3501/3501
     P2.3 876/876   P2.4 561/561   P2.5 141/141   P3.1 2/2
     P4.1 601/601   P5.4 1/1   P6.1 1401/1401   P7.1 7/7

   and `pull rev` is 0 on every row, which is the family's defining claim
   measured rather than assumed.

   **One traversal emitter parameterised by where values are deposited is
   buildable, and this is how much of it is shared.**  `dec_walk` in
   poc/codec/gen/rust_abi.py -- the tag match, the wire checks, the span
   arithmetic, 7.2's batching predicate, 7.3's flush on a foreign tag, the arena
   declaration -- is emitted ONCE and instantiated twice.  What differs between
   the families is three things and they are all outside it:

     * the `flush_*` macro body: a vtable call, or a record append;
     * the entry point's prologue and epilogue;
     * one parameter, `elemcall`, naming the non-leaf element decoder.

   `dec_walk` takes exactly one family-dependent argument.  That is the
   condition section 7.1 sets ("not two emitters that have to agree"), met.

3. THE INTERFACE ARGUMENT, WHICH IS A PROPERTY OF THE DESCRIPTOR AND NOT OF THIS
   MACHINE (R13)

     payload  push reverse   rev/elem   pull forward (32K)   pull forward (1 chunk)
     P2.2     3,501          7.002      16                   3
     P2.3       876          7.008      11                   3
     P2.4       561          7.013      13                   3
     P6.1     1,401          7.005       8                   3
     P4.1       601          3.005       5                   3
     P1.2         5          0.005       6                   3

   **Push's crossings are per ELEMENT; pull's are per MESSAGE.**  On P2.2 that is
   3,501 against 16, or against 3 if the host drains in one chunk.  Three is the
   floor for every payload in the set regardless of shape or size: parse,
   footprint, drain.

   The chunk size is the only knob a host has on that count, and it is a trade
   against how much of the response is materialised at once -- which is the bound
   section 7.1 gives the host in the first place.  A host on a 100 ns forward
   call and a 540 KB response pays 1.6 us at 32 KB chunks and 300 ns at one.

4. WHAT PULL COSTS THIS HOST: -5% TO +18% OF A PUSH DECODE, AND THE SIGN IS
   NEGATIVE ON THE SHAPE THE CONTROL PLANE MOVES

   Ratio to the push arm, min and max over all six timed runs:

     payload   pull (32K chunks)   pull-walk (no copy)   pull-opaque (control)
     P1.1      0.940 - 1.032       0.914 - 0.987         0.909 - 1.032
     P1.2      0.993 - 1.017       0.985 - 1.009         0.981 - 1.034
     P1.3      1.054 - 1.133       0.959 - 1.085         0.973 - 1.083
     P2.1      1.037 - 1.079       1.009 - 1.073         1.009 - 1.054
     P2.2      0.956 - 0.989       0.957 - 0.973         0.960 - 0.977
     P2.3      0.957 - 0.989       0.944 - 0.979         0.956 - 0.998
     P2.4      0.957 - 1.003       0.940 - 0.988         0.955 - 0.990
     P2.5      0.973 - 1.002       0.953 - 0.994         0.956 - 1.006
     P3.1      0.977 - 1.034       0.968 - 1.011         0.965 - 1.020
     P4.1      0.966 - 1.002       0.946 - 0.967         0.946 - 0.974
     P6.1      1.078 - 1.179       1.024 - 1.070         1.024 - 1.113
     P5.4      0.992 - 1.006       0.983 - 1.011         0.979 - 1.008

   **This is not the result the slice expected.**  The prediction on the way in
   was that at a 1.8 ns reverse call a family that trades reverse calls for a
   materialisation would lose, and on nine of twelve payloads the no-copy arm is
   at or below push.  What that says is that a push reverse call costs this host
   more than a crossing: the arm goes through a vtable slot reached from across
   the shared object, and the replay's equivalent is a local call over a buffer
   already in L2.

   THE TWO ROWS WHERE PULL LOSES, AND BOTH ARE THE SAME MECHANISM:
     * P1.3 (+5 to +13% with the copy), the ABSENT path.  Section 3 of the
       transcript has the reason and it is not a timing: the record stream is
       **63.6 times the wire** there, 38,488 B of records for 605 B of message,
       because a record carries the whole fixed group of an element that encodes
       to almost nothing.  Pull materialises what the GROUP is, push hands the
       same group over without storing it.
     * P6.1 (+8 to +18% with the copy), 1,401 records for 200 elements and a
       stream 1.52 times the wire.

   So pull's cost tracks the RATIO OF RECORD BYTES TO WIRE BYTES, which is a
   property of the shape (how sparse an element is), not of the payload size.
   Every payload where that ratio is below 1 is at or under push.

5. THE R5 CONTROL FOR THIS FAMILY, AND IT CLEARS

   A replay is host code calling host code, so rustc may inline `apply_*` and
   `add_*` into it; a push callback is reached through a vtable from across the
   shared-library boundary and never can be.  Subtracting the two families
   without checking that would charge an optimiser difference to the interface --
   the same hazard R5's second half already names for the no-boundary control.

   `core-ffi-pull-opaque` runs the identical replay with every call going through
   a `black_box`ed function pointer: no inlining, no devirtualisation, no
   constant propagation.  It measures the same as the plain walk arm on every
   payload (the two columns above are inside each other's spread everywhere).
   **The parity is not an inlining artifact.**

6. THE DECOMPOSITION, SO ANOTHER HOST CAN RE-PRICE IT

   ns per decode, medians from the same interleaved rounds:

     term          what it is                              P2.2        P1.3
     materialise   ak_parse_* alone, no object graph       345-361k    4.4-4.9k
     replay        walk - materialise                      1.27-1.29M  13.4-13.7k
     drain copy    pull - walk                             18-56k      1.3-1.9k

   As a fraction of a push decode: **materialise is 8.5% (P2.4) to 26% (P1.3),
   and 47% on P6.1; the drain copy is 1% to 12%.**

   Against the figure the branch is carrying: ABI v1 7.1 records that "the C#
   slice measured the pull form's intermediate as costing an estimated 12 to 19
   percent of a parse".  Both terms above are quoted rather than one, because
   "the intermediate" could mean either of them and the two differ by up to a
   factor of forty on this host.  What is NOT in doubt is that both are paid in
   full by a managed host and only the first by a native one.

   P5.4 is the control that bounds the materialise term from below: a 4 MB bulk
   decode parses in 18-19 ns, because the core records a span and copies nothing.
   Everything else on that row is the host's copy, which every arm pays.

7. WHAT THIS DOES NOT ESTABLISH

   * **It is not a verdict for the JVM, and it cannot be.**  What travels is the
     crossing counts (section 3) and the mechanism (section 4's ratio rule).  The
     ns figures are this container's.
   * Pull is built for the SEVEN roots of design/SHAPES.md and no others.
   * **Decision 11's unknown-field capture is not built for pull.**  The bag is a
     candidate and pull is a family; pricing one through the other would make
     neither answerable.  `ak_parse_*` skips unknown fields, which is what the
     default push path does too.
   * **`ak_bdr_reserve` is never called by these arms.**  Every timed pull decode
     grows the buffer from whatever the previous one left, which is the steady
     state; what a cold first parse costs is unmeasured.
   * The drain contract requires an 8-ALIGNED destination, because the host reads
     an ak_dfix_* out of it.  A JVM host reading through a VarHandle does not
     care; a host that cannot guarantee it would need unaligned loads, and
     nothing here measures what that costs.
   * A record larger than the chunk is AK_ERR_CAPACITY rather than a split
     record.  The largest a schema can produce is section 7.3's arena plus a
     header, so a host sized at AK_BDR_MIN_CHUNK never sees it -- and no test
     makes it happen.
   * **The no-boundary control is not extended to pull.**  `core-native` is the
     push traversal emitted into the host; there is no `core-native-pull`,
     because the question here is push against pull THROUGH THE SAME BOUNDARY,
     which R4's sharpened half says to ask as a delta between two arms in the
     same rounds -- and it is.
   * Encode is untouched.  Section 7.1 is about decode.
   * One machine, one microarchitecture, one thread.
